@page "/components/ai/markdownview"

<PageOutlet Url="components/ai/markdownview"
            Title="AiMarkdownView"
            Description="BitAiMarkdownView renders the Markdown an AI model writes - code blocks, tables, task lists, footnotes, mathematics and citation markers - safely: raw HTML escaped, script URLs neutralised, images under a policy." />

<DemoPage Name="AiMarkdownView"
          Description="BitAiMarkdownView renders a parsed Markdown document: the prose of an assistant message, with its code blocks, tables, task lists, footnotes, mathematics and inline citation markers. It takes an already-parsed document rather than a string, so the caller decides how parsing happens - BitAiStreamingMarkdown reuses an incremental parser across deltas, a static caller parses once with BitAiMarkdownParser. Model output is untrusted, so raw HTML is escaped unless it is opted into, link and image URLs of a scheme that can run script are neutralised, and images follow the image policy of the options. Fenced code in a language the app registered (a chart, a diagram) and mathematics defer to renderers of the app's own."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          SubEnums="componentSubEnums"
          CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
          GitHubAIUrl="MarkdownView/BitAiMarkdownView.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="AI/MarkdownView/BitAiMarkdownViewDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            To use this component, you need to install the
            <BitLink Href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Bit.BlazorUI.AI" Target="_blank">
                <BitTag Reversed
                        Text="Bit.BlazorUI.AI"
                        Color="BitColor.SecondaryBackground"
                        IconName="@BitIconName.NavigateExternalInline" />
            </BitLink>
            nuget package, as described in the Optional steps of the
            <BitLink Href="/getting-started">Getting started</BitLink> page.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            It needs no chat session: parse the Markdown with <b>BitAiMarkdownParser.Parse</b> and hand it the document. For text
            that is still arriving, use <BitLink Href="/components/ai/streamingmarkdown">BitAiStreamingMarkdown</BitLink>, which
            parses incrementally and renders through this view. The registered renderers (<b>BitAiRendererRegistry</b>) are a
            singleton: register them once, at startup, with <b>ConfigureRenderers</b> of <b>AddBitBlazorUIAIServices</b>.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            Security: raw HTML is shown as text unless both the parse options and the options in force allow it, which is only
            for Markdown you trust as much as your own code; links open in a new tab with <b>rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow"</b>
            and only the http, https, mailto, tel and ftp schemes; and a browser fetching an image URL a model was talked into
            leaks the reader's address - restrict images with the <b>BitAiImagePolicy</b> below. Accessibility: the headings are
            rendered one level below the Markdown's own (a <b>#</b> is an h2), so an answer never competes with the h1 of the page,
            and a table keeps its row and column semantics while it scrolls. See it at work in the
            <BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/streaming">Streaming</BitLink> and <BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/citations">Citations</BitLink> scenarios.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>
    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" Id="example1">
            <div>
                <b>Document</b> is the parsed Markdown to render; nothing is rendered while it is null. Parse it once with
                <b>BitAiMarkdownParser.Parse</b>: headings, emphasis, strikethrough, links, bare URLs, inline code and lists.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiMarkdownView Document="basicDocument" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Blocks" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" Id="example2">
            <div>
                Tables with aligned columns (scrolling in their own frame), block quotes, task lists, thematic breaks and fenced code -
                every fenced block is a <BitLink Href="/components/ai/codeblockview">BitAiCodeBlockView</BitLink> with its header,
                copy button and highlighting.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiMarkdownView Document="blocksDocument" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Streaming caret" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3">
            <div>
                <b>ShowCaret</b> draws a caret after the last paragraph while it is still open - a document parsed with
                <b>isComplete</b> false, the sign that more text is on its way. <b>BitAiUiOptions.ShowStreamingCaret</b> turns it off
                for every view at once.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiMarkdownView Document="openDocument" ShowCaret />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Footnotes & headings" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4">
            <div>
                Footnotes are listed after the text in the order they are first cited, each with a link back to every place that
                cites it. The anchors of the footnotes and the ids of the headings are named after the id of the view
                (<b>answer-bit-ai-h-setup</b> here), so two messages of one transcript never give the page two elements of one id.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiMarkdownView Id="answer" Document="footnotesDocument" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Mathematics" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5">
            <div>
                Dollar-delimited mathematics - <b>$inline$</b> and <b>$$display$$</b> - is left as its source, in monospace, with a
                <b>data-math</b> attribute a script can typeset from. <b>BitAiRendererRegistry.MathRenderer</b> replaces that
                fallback with a renderer of your own, handed a <b>BitAiMathContext</b>; the one here is a tiny typesetter of
                superscripts, subscripts and symbols, where an app would use KaTeX or MathJax. The registered registry is app-wide,
                so an app sets it once at startup; this page cascades a registry of its own to the views of these examples
                (a <b>CascadingValue</b> of a <b>BitAiRendererRegistry</b>), and the toggle sets the renderer on that one.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitToggle @bind-Value="typesetMath" @bind-Value:after="ToggleMathRenderer" Text="Use a math renderer of my own" />
            <CascadingValue Value="pageRenderers" IsFixed="true">
                <BitAiMarkdownView Document="mathDocument" />
            </CascadingValue>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Code languages" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
            <div>
                <b>BitAiRendererRegistry.RegisterCodeLanguage</b> renders the fenced blocks of one language - a chart, a diagram -
                with a renderer of your own in place of the code view, handed a <b>BitAiCodeBlockContext</b>. A half-written diagram
                is a syntax error, so a renderer waits for <b>IsStreaming</b> to turn false: the second block below has not been
                closed yet. This page registers a <b>bars</b> language on the registry it cascades to its views as it initializes.
            </div>
            <br />
            <CascadingValue Value="pageRenderers" IsFixed="true">
                <BitAiMarkdownView Document="barsDocument" />
                <br />
                <BitAiMarkdownView Document="openBarsDocument" />
            </CascadingValue>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Citations" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7">
            <div>
                A bracketed number in the text (<b>[1]</b>) is drawn as a citation chip when <b>Citations</b> holds a citation of
                that <b>Number</b>; one that matches none stays the text it was. <b>OnCitationClick</b> is raised when the user
                activates a chip. <b>BitAiUiOptions.InlineCitations</b> turns the chips off for every view at once.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiMarkdownView Document="citedDocument"
                               Citations="citations"
                               OnCitationClick="(BitAiCitation citation) => clickedSource = citation.Source.Title" />
            <br />
            <div>Activated: <b>@(clickedSource ?? "none yet")</b></div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Custom citation chip" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example8CsharpCode" Id="example8">
            <div>
                <b>BitAiRendererRegistry.CitationRenderer</b> draws the chips with a renderer of your own, handed a
                <b>BitAiCitationContext</b>: the <b>Citation</b>, and <b>OnActivate</b> - the <b>OnCitationClick</b> of the view - which
                the chip raises through <b>ActivateAsync()</b>, so it acts the way the built-in one does. The one here is a pill naming
                the site. The registered registry is app-wide, so an app sets it once at startup; this page cascades a registry of
                its own to the view below, and the toggle sets the renderer on that one - the chips of the example above, which
                reads the registered registry, stay as they are.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitToggle @bind-Value="customChips" @bind-Value:after="ToggleCitationRenderer" Text="Use a citation chip of my own" />
            <CascadingValue Value="pageRenderers" IsFixed="true">
                <BitAiMarkdownView Document="citedDocument"
                                   Citations="citations"
                                   OnCitationClick="(BitAiCitation citation) => chipSource = citation.Source.Title" />
            </CascadingValue>
            <br />
            <div>Activated: <b>@(chipSource ?? "none yet")</b></div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Images" RazorCode="@example9RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example9CsharpCode" Id="example9">
            <div>
                <b>BitAiUiOptions.Images</b> is the <b>BitAiImagePolicy</b> the images of the Markdown follow. <b>Allow</b> (the
                default) shows them all; <b>AllowListedHosts</b> shows the images of the listed hosts and a placeholder for the
                rest; <b>ClickToLoad</b> fetches nothing until the reader asks; <b>Block</b> shows the alternative text instead. A
                placeholder is a button that loads the image, named after its alternative text. A relative URL is resolved against
                the base address of the page before the policy is asked, so the host list here names this site's own host too.
                Inline <b>data:</b> images are fetched from nowhere, so they are shown under every policy but <b>Block</b> - unless
                <b>AllowDataImages</b> is off. Script-capable URL schemes are neutralised whatever the policy.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitChoiceGroup Label="Policy" Items="imageModeItems" @bind-Value="imageMode" @bind-Value:after="UpdateImageOptions" Horizontal />
            <BitToggle @bind-Value="allowDataImages" @bind-Value:after="UpdateImageOptions" Text="AllowDataImages" />
            <BitAiOptionsProvider UiOptions="imageOptions">
                <BitAiMarkdownView Document="imagesDocument" />
            </BitAiOptionsProvider>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Untrusted content" RazorCode="@example10RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example10CsharpCode" Id="example10">
            <div>
                Model output is untrusted. A <b>javascript:</b> link is neutralised to <b>#</b>, and raw HTML - inline or a block - is
                shown as the text it is. Rendering it takes <b>AllowRawHtml</b> in both the options the document is parsed with and
                the options in force (<b>BitAiUiOptions.Markdown</b>): turn it on only for Markdown you trust as much as your own code,
                since a model can be talked into writing a script tag.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitToggle @bind-Value="allowRawHtml" @bind-Value:after="UpdateRawHtml" Text="AllowRawHtml (trusted Markdown only)" />
            <BitAiOptionsProvider UiOptions="rawHtmlOptions">
                <BitAiMarkdownView Document="untrustedDocument" />
            </BitAiOptionsProvider>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Markdown options" RazorCode="@example11RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example11CsharpCode" Id="example11">
            <div>
                <b>BitAiMarkdownOptions</b> picks the extensions the parser reads: pipe tables, task lists, strikethrough, bare URLs
                as links, mathematics, footnotes, citation markers and heading ids are on by default, and
                <b>SoftBreaksAsHardBreaks</b> - for chat models that write one line per sentence and mean the breaks - is off. Hand
                them to <b>BitAiMarkdownParser.Parse</b>; a BitAiStreamingMarkdown reads them from <b>BitAiUiOptions.Markdown</b>.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitStack Horizontal Wrap AutoHeight Gap="0.5rem 1.5rem">
                <BitToggle @bind-Value="parseTables" @bind-Value:after="Reparse" Text="Tables" />
                <BitToggle @bind-Value="parseTaskLists" @bind-Value:after="Reparse" Text="TaskLists" />
                <BitToggle @bind-Value="parseStrikethrough" @bind-Value:after="Reparse" Text="Strikethrough" />
                <BitToggle @bind-Value="parseAutoLinks" @bind-Value:after="Reparse" Text="AutoLinks" />
                <BitToggle @bind-Value="parseMath" @bind-Value:after="Reparse" Text="Math" />
                <BitToggle @bind-Value="parseSoftBreaks" @bind-Value:after="Reparse" Text="SoftBreaksAsHardBreaks" />
            </BitStack>
            <br />
            <BitAiMarkdownView Document="optionsDocument" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example12RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example12CsharpCode" Id="example12">
            <div>
                <b>BitParams</b> with a <b>BitAiMarkdownViewParams</b> sets the defaults of every Markdown view inside it - here the
                styles of its links and quotes - and a parameter of the view itself still wins.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitParams Parameters="markdownParams">
                <BitAiMarkdownView Document="paramsDocument" />
                <br />
                <BitAiMarkdownView Document="paramsDocument" Styles="@(new() { Link = "color: mediumseagreen;" })" />
            </BitParams>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example13RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example13CsharpCode" Id="example13">
            <div>
                <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> reach the root, <b>Styles</b> and <b>Classes</b> every element the view renders - each
                under a class of its own, so the prose styles never reach into what a registered renderer draws. The public
                <b>--bit-AiMarkdownView-*</b> variables (see the CSS variables table) restyle every view under the element they are
                set on, the ones a reasoning panel or an artifact renders inside a message included.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                @* A plain element of this page for the scoped ::deep rules of the custom classes to anchor on. *@
                <BitAiMarkdownView Document="styleDocument" Style="padding: 0.5rem; border: 1px dashed dodgerblue;" />
                <br />
                <BitAiMarkdownView Document="styleDocument" Class="custom-class" />
                <br />
                <BitAiMarkdownView Document="styleDocument"
                                   Styles="@(new() { Heading = "color: tomato;", InlineCode = "color: tomato;", Quote = "font-style: italic;" })" />
                <br />
                <BitAiMarkdownView Document="styleDocument"
                                   Classes="@(new() { Root = "custom-root", Link = "custom-link", ListItem = "custom-item" })" />
                <br />
                <div style="--bit-AiMarkdownView-heading-color: rebeccapurple; --bit-AiMarkdownView-link-color: rebeccapurple; --bit-AiMarkdownView-quote-border-color: rebeccapurple; --bit-AiMarkdownView-code-background: #ede9fe; --bit-AiMarkdownView-block-spacing: 0.5rem;">
                    <BitAiMarkdownView Document="styleDocument" />
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example14RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example14CsharpCode" Id="example14">
            <div>
                In right-to-left text the Markdown reads from the right - list markers, the bar of a quote, the columns of a table
                and its end-aligned cells - whether the direction comes from <b>Dir</b> or from a <b>dir</b> attribute around it.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiMarkdownView Dir="BitDir.Rtl" Document="rtlDocument" />
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>
</DemoPage>
